The `STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` at line 553 does `switch (string_as_int32(p) | 0x20202020)` which reads 4 bytes from `p`. 

The bug is: when a header line is very short (less than 4 bytes between start and end), the `STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` reads 4 bytes which could go beyond the buffer boundary if the header is near the end of the buffer.

Looking at the `HEADER` macro:
```c
#define HEADER(hdr)                                                            \
    ({                                                                         \
        if (UNLIKELY(end - sizeof(hdr) + 1 < p))                               \
            continue;                                                          \
        ...
    })
```

The check `end - sizeof(hdr) + 1 < p` is supposed to prevent reading past the end, but this only happens inside the switch case bodies, not at the `STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` itself.

The issue is that `STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` at line 553 reads 4 bytes starting at `p`, but there's no check that `p` has at least 4 bytes before `end`.

For the fuzzer, the buffer is `data_copy[32767]` which is a static (global) buffer. If the input data puts a header near the very end of the meaningful data (near `buffer_end`), but `buffer_end` happens to be past the global buffer boundary, the 4-byte read in `STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` could read beyond the global buffer.

Wait, let me re-read the fuzzer code:
```c
static char data_copy[32767] = {0};
if (length > sizeof(data_copy))
    length = sizeof(data_copy);
memcpy(data_copy, data, length);
```

And `parse_headers` is called with:
```c
parse_headers(helper, buffer, helper->buffer->value + helper->buffer->len)
```

So `buffer_end = data_copy + length`.

