But wait, the fuzzer limits length to sizeof(data_copy) = 32767. So buffer_end = data_copy + 32767 at most. The issue is that if a header start is at an offset where there are fewer than 4 bytes remaining in the buffer, the STRING_SWITCH_L read of 4 bytes would read past the end.

Actually, let me reconsider. The `n_headers` pairs track (start, end) of each header line. The `start` is the character after `\r\n`, and `end` is the next `\r`. So if we have a very short header (like 1-3 bytes) that ends right at buffer_end with no `\r` found (the `!next_hdr` case), we go to `process`.

